<h2>The eviction petition and the objections</h2>
<p>The petitioner sought eviction of the tenant from Shop No. 1 in the Shambhu Nath Building at Gandhi Nagar, Shahdara, pleading a bona fide need: his daughter-in-law wished to start a business in ladies&rsquo; readymade garments and interior designing. The petition travelled the summary route of Section 25-B before the Additional Rent Controller, Shahdara (RC ARC No. 153/2020).</p>
<p>The tenant&rsquo;s application for leave to defend attacked the foundation rather than the need. He denied any landlord-tenant relationship with the petitioner and said he had never paid him rent. He pointed to the petitioner&rsquo;s own paper trail: a 2010 sale deed in favour of the petitioner&rsquo;s son recording property number IX/1393 instead of IX/1392, followed by a rectification order of 2019 and a rectification deed of 2020; a 2008 relinquishment deed recording an earlier sale to a third party; an earlier civil suit in which a much higher rent had been claimed; and pending FIRs for cheating and forgery said not to have been disclosed. By order of 20 August 2024, the Controller rejected the objections on bona fide need and alternative accommodation but granted leave to defend on the landlord-tenant relationship. The landlord went in revision.</p>

<h2>The rival submissions</h2>
<p>For the landlord it was argued that the Controller overlooked the registered sale and rectification deeds, exceeded the limited inquiry permissible under Section 14(1)(e) by probing the validity of title, and wrongly treated a challenge mounted by a different tenant as one made by the respondent. The rent receipts and the respondent&rsquo;s earlier stand on the Act&rsquo;s applicability were said to have been ignored. For the tenant it was answered that the Controller&rsquo;s order was detailed and reasoned, that leave to defend involves only a prima facie assessment, and that the documentary discrepancies and pending proceedings raised genuinely triable issues.</p>

<h2>The Court&rsquo;s reasoning</h2>
<p>Justice Shankar began with the boundaries of the jurisdiction. Applying <em>Sarla Ahuja v. United India Insurance</em>, <em>Hindustan Petroleum v. Dilbahar Singh</em> and <em>Abid-Ul-Islam v. Inder Sain Dua</em> — the last quoted for the proposition that the scope of interference by the High Court is very restrictive — together with this Court&rsquo;s decisions in <em>Pankaj Pahwa v. Prem Wati</em> and <em>Sanjeev Hiranandani v. Sunny Grover</em>, and the Supreme Court&rsquo;s exposition of leave to defend in <em>Precision Steel &amp; Engineering Works v. Prem Deva Niranjan Deva Tayal</em>, the Court held that revision under the proviso to Section 25-B(8) is not a rehearing.</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>No final title ruling.</strong> The Controller did not decide ownership; in eviction proceedings the landlord need only show a better title than the tenant.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Discrepancies count.</strong> Noticing that the petitioner&rsquo;s own documents contained inconsistencies was within jurisdiction, not in excess of it.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Trial issues stay at trial.</strong> Rent receipts and the pending criminal matters are for evidence, not for revisional determination.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Conjunctive requirements.</strong> A prima facie finding of bona fide need cannot cure a triable doubt about the landlord-tenant relationship, because Section 14(1)(e)&rsquo;s elements operate together.</div>
</div>
<p>The point about the other tenant&rsquo;s proceedings did not carry the day either: the Controller&rsquo;s conclusion rested on the other discrepancies as well, so the error, if any, was not decisive. The revision petition was dismissed with no order as to costs, the Court making clear that its observations were confined to the revisional scope and that the Controller must decide the eviction petition uninfluenced by them.</p>

<h2>Takeaways for landlords and tenants</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Landlords filing under Section 14(1)(e) should audit their title chain before filing. Rectification deeds, old relinquishments and historic litigation do not defeat the petition, but unexplained inconsistencies hand the tenant a triable issue at the leave stage.</p>
  <p>Tenants seeking leave to defend should anchor the application in documents already on the record — the landlord&rsquo;s own papers are the most persuasive source of a genuine dispute.</p>
  <p>Both sides should remember that the revision under Section 25-B(8) tests legality, not persuasion: the High Court will not substitute its view where the Controller&rsquo;s order is a possible one on the material.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article describes a revisional judgment of the Delhi High Court for general information. Leave to defend is a threshold determination; the underlying eviction petition remains to be tried on its own evidence.</div>
